WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.502
We finish the module by returning to the complete RAG workflow.

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:03.917
Start with documents. Preserve metadata.

00:00:03.917 --> 00:00:05.692
Create chunks. Generate embeddings. Store or index those vectors.

00:00:05.692 --> 00:00:07.152
When a question arrives, embed the query, retrieve the best evidence, build a grounded prompt,

00:00:07.152 --> 00:00:08.512
generate the answer, and show the sources.

00:00:08.512 --> 00:00:10.002
Then evaluate the system. [tts-break]

00:00:10.002 --> 00:00:10.332
If retrieval is weak, improve retrieval.

00:00:10.332 --> 00:00:14.277
If the correct evidence is present but generation is weak, investigate the prompt and model behavior.

00:00:14.277 --> 00:00:15.577
If the system becomes too large for NumPy, move to a vector store.

00:00:15.577 --> 00:00:18.982
The most important takeaway is that the architecture remains understandable even when the tools change.

00:00:18.982 --> 00:00:21.862
You should now be able to replace our student-support dataset with a small collection from your own work

00:00:21.862 --> 00:00:23.172
and build the same pipeline.

00:00:23.172 --> 00:00:23.474
That is the practical foundation of RAG.
